        <briefdescription>
<para>Newmark-beta time integration at one point: the acceleration and the velocity at the end of an increment from the displacement increment. </para>
        </briefdescription>
        <detaileddescription>
<para>Solves the Newmark relations     <formula id="98">\[  \Delta u = \Delta t\, v_n + \tfrac{\Delta t^2}{2} \left[ (1 - 2\beta)\, a_n + 2\beta\, a_{n+1} \right],
  \qquad
  v_{n+1} = v_n + \Delta t \left[ (1 - \gamma)\, a_n + \gamma\, a_{n+1} \right]
\]</formula> for <formula id="99">$ a_{n+1} $</formula> and <formula id="100">$ v_{n+1} $</formula>, component by component, and returns the derivative <formula id="101">$ \partial a_{n+1} / \partial \Delta u = 1 / (\beta \Delta t^2) $</formula>, which the host&apos;s tangent needs for the inertia term.</para>
<para>Two cases add no inertia:<itemizedlist>
<listitem><para><formula id="102">$ \beta = 0 $</formula>: no time integration (a quasi-static analysis), which is at rest: the velocity and the acceleration are zero, whatever they were before (e.g. after a dynamic step), and so is the derivative.</para>
</listitem><listitem><para>A zero-length increment, <formula id="103">$ \Delta t = 0 $</formula>: nothing moves in zero time, so the velocity and the acceleration are left as they are ( <formula id="104">$ v_{n+1} = v_n $</formula>, <formula id="105">$ a_{n+1} = a_n $</formula>), whatever <formula id="106">$ \Delta u $</formula> is, and the derivative is zero. The inertial force of the unchanged acceleration remains in the host&apos;s residual. A host that integrates in time skips such an increment, or evaluates at it only to compute a state at rest in time (e.g. the acceleration of its own nodes from equilibrium).</para>
</listitem></itemizedlist>
</para>
<para><parameterlist kind="templateparam"><parameteritem>
<parameternamelist>
<parametername>nDim</parametername>
</parameternamelist>
<parameterdescription>
<para>Number of components. </para>
